{"authored" [{"question" "q-347888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(x-1),\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=0$ to approximate $y(1).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y(1)≈$  \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "2d6ae68523c2f966bdc5b6766936ca10e76e5a1c7503b0e51125de7ac86be0c1" "basis" 1265 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-347888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=0$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)(x-0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(0)=2.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)=y(x-1),$ so\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=2(0-1)=-2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(0)$ and ${y}^{′}(0)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)(x-0) \\\\  & =2+(-2)(x-0) \\\\  & =2-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(1)$ using $L(1).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & ≈L(1) \\\\  & =2-2(1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "2d6ae68523c2f966bdc5b6766936ca10e76e5a1c7503b0e51125de7ac86be0c1" "basis" 1265 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-347888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ccdf2665e3afb1bacb9f9fc7d2163dfc8c3bf6bfd4d4535f4ccc0d0e48d110db" "basis" 1265 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-347888" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "2d6ae68523c2f966bdc5b6766936ca10e76e5a1c7503b0e51125de7ac86be0c1" "basis" 1265 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-353287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{y}},\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ to approximate $y(\\frac{3}{4}).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y(\\frac{3}{4})≈$  \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "2d6ae68523c2f966bdc5b6766936ca10e76e5a1c7503b0e51125de7ac86be0c1" "basis" 1265 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-353287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=\\frac{1}{2}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(\\frac{1}{2})+{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})(x-\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{4}.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{y}},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and ${y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(\\frac{1}{2})+{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+(-1)(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(\\frac{3}{4})$ using $L(\\frac{3}{4}).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{3}{4}) & ≈L(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "2d6ae68523c2f966bdc5b6766936ca10e76e5a1c7503b0e51125de7ac86be0c1" "basis" 1265 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-353287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ccdf2665e3afb1bacb9f9fc7d2163dfc8c3bf6bfd4d4535f4ccc0d0e48d110db" "basis" 1265 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-353287" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "2d6ae68523c2f966bdc5b6766936ca10e76e5a1c7503b0e51125de7ac86be0c1" "basis" 1265 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-353287" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "2d6ae68523c2f966bdc5b6766936ca10e76e5a1c7503b0e51125de7ac86be0c1" "basis" 1265 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-536" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = 2xy, \\qquad y(1)=2,\n$$\n\nuse the tangent line approximation at $x=1$ to approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/example-536.json" "file_sha256" "c71e8615f7e325af6d4423efba38c2d11b31dd0bd49e948098e4e842f028c163" "basis" 1265 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-536" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(1) = 2.$ We also know that $y'(x) = 2xy,$ so\n\n$$\ny'(1) = 2(1)(2) = 4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(1)$ and $y'(1)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =2+(4)(x-1) \\\\  & =2+4x-4 \\\\  & =4x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right)$ using $L\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right).$ We get\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac 3 2 \\right)\\approx L\\left(\\dfrac 3 2 \\right) = 4\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right) - 2 = 4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/example-536.json" "file_sha256" "c71e8615f7e325af6d4423efba38c2d11b31dd0bd49e948098e4e842f028c163" "basis" 1265 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7700" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' =7x-2y, \\qquad y(1)=-4\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $\\Delta y$ in the table below if the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1}{10}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ |  |  | $\\, ? \\,$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/example-7700.json" "file_sha256" "cbdd9515c4c9196e9e543d441ec37be55c555971d72cd8773a7e81aacf17c68e" "basis" 1265 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7700" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=-4,$ we fill $y=-4$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-4$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =7x-2y \\\\  & =7\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-4$ | $15$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =15\\cdot \\frac{1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-4$ | $15$ | $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/example-7700.json" "file_sha256" "cbdd9515c4c9196e9e543d441ec37be55c555971d72cd8773a7e81aacf17c68e" "basis" 1265 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-9340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' =2y^2-x^2, \\qquad y(1)=3\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/example-9340.json" "file_sha256" "1dde178a5287557916c8f15138b8b1606d0bd13852dd6e9120e578bb270e4be3" "basis" 1265 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-9340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2-1 = 1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {3}^{2}-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =18-1 \\\\  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =17\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ | $17$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$ and $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =3+17 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ | $17$ |\n| $2$ | $20$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2) \\approx 20.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/example-9340.json" "file_sha256" "1dde178a5287557916c8f15138b8b1606d0bd13852dd6e9120e578bb270e4be3" "basis" 1265 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-116791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}x+2,\\,y(0)=-1.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=0$ to approximate $y(0.5).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-116791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=0$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)(x-0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(0)=-1.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)={y}^{2}x+2,$ so\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=(-1)^{2}\\cdot 0+2=2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(0)$ and ${y}^{′}(0)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)(x-0) \\\\  & =-1+(2)(x-0) \\\\  & =2x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(0.5)$ using $L(0.5).$ We get\n\n$$\ny(0.5)≈L(0.5)=2(0.5)-1=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-116791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e73482b6e0a28b530b58e6b3712f99516645251f67d88afb474d0e192eeda036"} {"question" "q-116791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-116791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-116791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-347888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(x-1),\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=0$ to approximate $y(1).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y(1)≈$  \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-347888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=0$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)(x-0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(0)=2.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)=y(x-1),$ so\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=2(0-1)=-2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(0)$ and ${y}^{′}(0)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)(x-0) \\\\  & =2+(-2)(x-0) \\\\  & =2-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(1)$ using $L(1).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & ≈L(1) \\\\  & =2-2(1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-347888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e73482b6e0a28b530b58e6b3712f99516645251f67d88afb474d0e192eeda036"} {"question" "q-347888" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-347888" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-83382" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,y(3)=e.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=3$ to approximate $y(4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-83382" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(3)+{y}^{′}(3)(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(3)=e.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,$ so\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(3)=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(3)$ and ${y}^{′}(3)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(3)+{y}^{′}(3)(x-3) \\\\  & =e+(3)(x-3) \\\\  & =3x+e-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(4)$ using $L(4).$ We get\n\n$$\ny(4)≈L(4)=3(4)+e-9=e+3\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-83382" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e73482b6e0a28b530b58e6b3712f99516645251f67d88afb474d0e192eeda036"} {"question" "q-83382" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-83382" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "2e+1"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "3e"] ["math" "e+3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-83382" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "e+3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-53846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,y(1)=2.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=1$ to approximate $y(1.5).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-53846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(1)=2.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{x},$ so\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(1)$ and ${y}^{′}(1)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)(x-1) \\\\  & =2+(1)(x-1) \\\\  & =x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(1.5)$ using $L(1.5).$ We get\n\n$$\ny(1.5)≈L(1.5)=1.5+1=2.5.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-53846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e73482b6e0a28b530b58e6b3712f99516645251f67d88afb474d0e192eeda036"} {"question" "q-53846" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-53846" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.5"] ["math" "1.0"] ["math" "1.5"] ["math" "2.0"] ["math" "2.5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-53846" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-353287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{y}},\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ to approximate $y(\\frac{3}{4}).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y(\\frac{3}{4})≈$  \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-353287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=\\frac{1}{2}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(\\frac{1}{2})+{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})(x-\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{4}.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{y}},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and ${y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(\\frac{1}{2})+{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+(-1)(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(\\frac{3}{4})$ using $L(\\frac{3}{4}).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{3}{4}) & ≈L(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-353287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e73482b6e0a28b530b58e6b3712f99516645251f67d88afb474d0e192eeda036"} {"question" "q-353287" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-353287" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-86900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x-4y,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $$ | $$ | $\\,?\\,$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-86900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=2,$ we fill $y=2$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3x-4y \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-86900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e73482b6e0a28b530b58e6b3712f99516645251f67d88afb474d0e192eeda036"} {"question" "q-86900" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-86900" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-5"] ["math" "-\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-86900" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{5}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-353294" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+2y,\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Fill in the empty box in the column for $Δy$ in the table below using this method. Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-353294" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=1,$ we fill $y=1$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+2y \\\\  & =1+2(1) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-353294" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e73482b6e0a28b530b58e6b3712f99516645251f67d88afb474d0e192eeda036"} {"question" "q-353294" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-353294" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-347894" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}-1,\\,y(-1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Fill in the empty box in the column for $Δy$ in the table below using this method. Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $$ | $$ | {{field-1}} |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-347894" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, because the initial condition is $y(-1)=2,$ we fill $y=2$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x{y}^{2}-1 \\\\  & =(-1)(2)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =-5(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-\\frac{5}{4}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-347894" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e73482b6e0a28b530b58e6b3712f99516645251f67d88afb474d0e192eeda036"} {"question" "q-347894" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-347894" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{5}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-116878" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y-7x,\\,y(1)=3\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{6}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $$ | $$ | $\\,?\\,$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/ques