--- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =-2(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-3$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-353298" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e73482b6e0a28b530b58e6b3712f99516645251f67d88afb474d0e192eeda036"} {"question" "q-353298" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-353298" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-116886" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2xy,\\,y(-1)=3\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\,?\\,$ | $$ | $$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-116886" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, because the initial condition is $y(-1)=3,$ we fill $y=3$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy \\\\  & =2(-1)(3) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ | $-6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ | $-6$ | $-3$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =3+(-3) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ | $-6$ | $-3$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(-\\frac{1}{2})≈0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-116886" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e73482b6e0a28b530b58e6b3712f99516645251f67d88afb474d0e192eeda036"} {"question" "q-116886" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-116886" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-116886" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-353307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\sqrt{3x+4}+y,\\,y(4)=-1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method with step size $Δx=1.$ Fill in the empty box in the table below using this method. Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |\n| $5$ | {{field-1}} | $$ | $$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-353307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, because the initial condition is $y(4)=-1,$ we fill $y=-1$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt{3x+4}+y \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3(4)+4}+(-1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16}-1 \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $-1$ | $3$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3(1) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =-1+3 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $5$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(5)≈2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-353307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e73482b6e0a28b530b58e6b3712f99516645251f67d88afb474d0e192eeda036"} {"question" "q-353307" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-353307" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-353325" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x(2y-1),\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(1).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y(1)≈$  \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-353325" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\nΔx=1-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x(2y-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 1-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈\\frac{5}{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-353325" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e73482b6e0a28b530b58e6b3712f99516645251f67d88afb474d0e192eeda036"} {"question" "q-353325" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-353325" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-86975" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=5x+6y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-86975" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5x+6y \\\\  & =5\\cdot (0)+6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(6)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+6 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n| $1$ | $7$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈7.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-86975" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e73482b6e0a28b530b58e6b3712f99516645251f67d88afb474d0e192eeda036"} {"question" "q-86975" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-86975" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-86975" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-116773" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2xy,\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ |  | $$ | $$ |\n| $2$ | $\\,?\\,$ | $$ | $$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-116773" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=1,$ we fill $y=1$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy \\\\  & =2\\cdot (1)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-116773" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e73482b6e0a28b530b58e6b3712f99516645251f67d88afb474d0e192eeda036"} {"question" "q-116773" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-116773" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "q-116773" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/state.json" "file_sha256" "6ac5f0a7eb3e075a37f32f6b345efee97f04aa3c15cd1323af6ed7cd91e43997"} {"question" "e-536" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = 2xy, \\qquad y(1)=2,\n$$\n\nuse the tangent line approximation at $x=1$ to approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/example-536.json" "file_sha256" "c71e8615f7e325af6d4423efba38c2d11b31dd0bd49e948098e4e842f028c163"} {"question" "e-536" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(1) = 2.$ We also know that $y'(x) = 2xy,$ so\n\n$$\ny'(1) = 2(1)(2) = 4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(1)$ and $y'(1)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =2+(4)(x-1) \\\\  & =2+4x-4 \\\\  & =4x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right)$ using $L\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right).$ We get\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac 3 2 \\right)\\approx L\\left(\\dfrac 3 2 \\right) = 4\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right) - 2 = 4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/example-536.json" "file_sha256" "c71e8615f7e325af6d4423efba38c2d11b31dd0bd49e948098e4e842f028c163"} {"question" "e-7700" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' =7x-2y, \\qquad y(1)=-4\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $\\Delta y$ in the table below if the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1}{10}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ |  |  | $\\, ? \\,$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/example-7700.json" "file_sha256" "cbdd9515c4c9196e9e543d441ec37be55c555971d72cd8773a7e81aacf17c68e"} {"question" "e-7700" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=-4,$ we fill $y=-4$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-4$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =7x-2y \\\\  & =7\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-4$ | $15$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =15\\cdot \\frac{1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-4$ | $15$ | $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/example-7700.json" "file_sha256" "cbdd9515c4c9196e9e543d441ec37be55c555971d72cd8773a7e81aacf17c68e"} {"question" "e-9340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' =2y^2-x^2, \\qquad y(1)=3\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/example-9340.json" "file_sha256" "1dde178a5287557916c8f15138b8b1606d0bd13852dd6e9120e578bb270e4be3"} {"question" "e-9340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2-1 = 1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {3}^{2}-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =18-1 \\\\  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =17\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ | $17$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$ and $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =3+17 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ | $17$ |\n| $2$ | $20$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2) \\approx 20.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039970/example-9340.json" "file_sha256" "1dde178a5287557916c8f15138b8b1606d0bd13852dd6e9120e578bb270e4be3"}] "usage" {"q-353287" [] "q-53846" [] "e-7700" [] "q-86975" [] "e-536" [] "q-86900" [] "e-9340" [] "q-347888" []} "basis" 1398 "retractions" [[17592186079020 :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$y^{′} = \\frac{1}{x}, y(1) = 2$.\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x = 1$ to approximate $y(1.5)$."] [17592186079128 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$y^{′} = 3x - 4y, y(1) = 2$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx = \\frac{1}{2}$?\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ |  |  | $$? |"] [17592186079131 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$y^{′} = 5x + 6y, y(0) = 1$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(1)$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186077332 17592186077333 17592186077334 17592186077335 17592186077336 17592186077980 17592186077981 17592186077982 17592186077983 17592186077984 17592186077998 17592186077999 17592186